  "audio": "job/narration.mp3",
  "headline": "ACCELERATING MULTI-TEACHER DISTILLATION",
  "facts": {
    "paper": {
      "abstract": "Multi-teacher on-policy distillation consolidates expert capabilities by supervising student-generated trajectories, whereas task arithmetic offers a training-free alternative through linear parameter combinations. We view the linear combination of student and expert parameters as a single differentiable model and propose P^2-MOPD, which jointly optimizes the mixing coefficients and the student weights under the same on-policy distillation objective. Unlike task arithmetic, which stays within the span of the frozen student and expert parameters for any choice of merging coefficients, P^2-MOPD",
      "upvotes": 42,
      "authors": ["Sinuo Wang", "Jiacheng Lu", "Zeyu Zhang", "Maojia Song", "Rui Sun", "Beidi Luan"]
    }
  },
  "claims": [
    "Multi-teacher on-policy distillation (MOPD) consolidates expert capabilities by supervising student-generated trajectories.",
    "MOPD's pipeline is heavy and it does not recover all domains evenly.",
    "P^2-MOPD views the linear combination of student and expert parameters as a single differentiable model.",
    "P^2-MOPD jointly optimizes the mixing coefficients and the student weights under the on-policy distillation objective.",
    "P^2-MOPD uses experts as parameter priors without confining the student to the span of frozen student and expert parameters.",
    "For a 1.7B model, P^2-MOPD achieved 94.7% average recovery, compared to MOPD's 80.9%.",
    "Expert parameters, kept as learnable priors throughout distillation, guide where the student moves, making expert weights a source of supervision."
  ],
